Wait, there's no trailing `\` on the first line `\n{BOLD}...{RESET}\n\` — yes there is (`{RESET}\n\`). Line 2: `     \x20 {DIM}No...then\n\`. Hmm — Rust string continuation with `\` at end of line strips the newline AND leading whitespace of the next line. So `\n\` at end of line 1 then line 2 starts with spaces then `\x20`. After a line-continuation backslash, leading whitespace on the next line is stripped, so `\x20` renders as a single space. Let me verify this renders sensibly: 

Line1: `\n{BOLD}  ── Last Validation ──{RESET}\n` (after `\` continuation, the leading whitespace of line 2 stripped)
Line2: `\x20 {DIM}No validation data yet — run ...` → renders as ` No validation data yet — run /risk snapshot first, then`
Line3: `\x20 {DIM}trigger a watch failure (or /risk validate) to record it.`

So output:
```

── Last Validation ──
 No validation data yet — run /risk snapshot first, then
 trigger a watch failure (or /risk validate) to record it.
